Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> And presumably you should begin every turn of your conversations with
> ".i mi'e .aionys." Otherwise, you can't use "mi" because in the text
> you are expanding it (not your own text) "mi" already refers to a
> different person. But I don't think I have noticed you doing that.

Semantics and not syntax.  Lojban should not pretend to have unambigious=20
semantics (regardless of how many speakers are involved).  However, by=20
convention, absent of explicit mi'e, the referent of "mi" is the=20
speaker, and is not determined by the text that it is part of.  ("Text"=20
being a syntactic and not a semantic concept).

The problem I thought was being discussed was how to identify something=20
as syntactically part of someone else's text.  What it "means" (what all=20
the variables refer to) is a separate problem that is probably insoluble.

(In the pre-xorlo language, what I mean by "le broda" and what you mean=20
by "le broda" might two completely independent things whether they are=20
part of one text or not.  My mind is not your mind, and what I have in=20
mind almost certainly is NOT the exact same concept as what you have in=20
mind, though we might use the same description "le broda" to describe=20
the same referent.  I have no idea what xorlo does to this (and probably=20
don't want to try to know it %^).)

